Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: a learned backhaul station row known by its mode and MAC, not by having no radio

A backhaul station that an agent reports only in Device Information (an adapter such as EMOSA) is
kept as a backhaul-STA row whose radio identifier is the station's own MAC. Two rules assumed no
radio of the agent has that identifier: the restore of such a row reloaded from the database
(which keeps neither its mode nor its station), and the removal of a row whose station is no
longer reported. A station may share its MAC with its radio's identifier (EasyMesh allows it; a
hwsim pod's 2.4 GHz station does). Its reloaded row was then never restored, and the station was
learned again as a second row with the same key; a row no longer reported was never removed.

The removal also ran only when the response reported no station at all, so an agent that moved
its backhaul to a station on another band kept its old station's row beside the new one: two
backhaul STA rows, the first of which SteerWiFiBackhaul() then named.

Now a reloaded row is the station's when its radio is unknown, as before, or when its BSSID is
the station's reported parent, which no BSS of the agent's own can be. Every learned station row
whose station this response does not report goes, also when it reports another station. A
reloaded row of a station that shares its radio's identifier is an AP-mode row of that radio,
which the Operational BSS TLV's stale-BSS rule removes once the radio no longer reports it.

Platform-independent (completes the lab's 0236); not for upstream on its own.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [completes 0236, a lab patch]

